@startuml proto-fm-biphase-timing
' ===========================================================================
' proto-fm-biphase-timing.puml - (c) the bi-phase timing the act is built on.
'
' *** STATUS: designed WIP. The numbers below are the DESIGN's constants, from
' firmware/bmc_frame.pe and tb/tb_pe_soc_bmc.v on fw-timing-protocols. They are
' NOT measured, because the act is 7/7 RED. A timing figure for a passing act
' would carry measured numbers; this one carries the targets the checks assert
' against, and says so, because a figure that cannot tell the two apart is a
' figure that overstates the evidence.
' ===========================================================================

title FM0/FM1 bi-phase - the timing the act is built on\n<font size=12>DESIGN constants, not measured: this act is 7/7 RED on fw-timing-protocols</font>

hide stereotype
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequence {
  ArrowColor #33555
  LifeLineBorderColor #33555
  LifeLineBackgroundColor #FAFAFA
  ParticipantBorderColor #33555
  ParticipantBackgroundColor #F4F7FB
  ParticipantFontName monospaced
  ActorFontName monospaced
  NoteFontName monospaced
  GroupFontName monospaced
}

participant "ENCODER\nthe TB, or the\nfirmware on OUT_PAD" as ENC
participant "the wire\nno clock on it" as WIRE
participant "DECODER\ntimestamps, it does\nnot synchronise" as DEC
participant "the 1 us counter" as TICK

== why the half-interval is a WHOLE number of microseconds ==

note over ENC, DEC
  <b>THE BIT PERIOD IS 4 us: TWO 2 us HALF-INTERVALS.</b>
  ...
  And that is not tidiness. The decoder TIMESTAMPS the level on the 1 us counter
  and compares it with the level recorded at the last CHANGE, and <b>that comparison
  is only exact if every transition lands on a tick.</b> A bit period that is not a
  multiple of the counter's unit would make the decoder's OWN ARITHMETIC the least
  accurate thing in the act - and a decoder whose timing is its weakest part is not
  a decoder that has been measured, it is one that has been assumed.
  ...
  The half-interval is therefore <b>COUNTED, not waited on:</b> 120 clocks = 2 us
  exactly at 60 MHz. A loop waiting on the free-running tick would inherit the
  phase residual the WS2812 act documented - up to a full microsecond, which here
  is HALF A HALF-INTERVAL.
end note

== the three symbols, as waveforms ==

group symbol 0 - the same in FM0 and FM1
  ENC -> WIRE : |  transition   (the middle)
  WIRE -> DEC : the decoder timestamps this at a TICK
  note over DEC
    a 0 <b>HAS</b> a transition at the END of its interval -
    and it has one in BOTH encodings, so the 0 is the
    symbol a receiver can get right first.
  end note
end group

group symbol 1 in FM0 - the extra transition is the whole difference
  ENC -> WIRE : |  transition   (the middle)   <-- FM0's
  note over DEC
    a data transition <b>INSIDE</b> the first half-interval is
    what says "FM0". A sample-based edge detector cannot
    see the difference at all: all three symbols have a
    transition somewhere.
  end note
end group

group symbol 1 in FM1 - no transition anywhere in the bit
  note over WIRE
    <b>NO transition at all inside this bit.</b>
  end note
  note over DEC
    <b>AND THIS IS A CLEAN SQUARE WAVE IN A RUN OF ONES</b> -
    one transition per bit, in the middle, from which the
    decoder recovers its clock exactly.
    ...
    <b>THE FIRST VERSION OF THE TESTBENCH CLAIMED THAT A RUN
    OF ONES IN FM1 WAS THE "NO TRANSITIONS" STREAM AND
    THEREFORE NOT A DECODABLE FRAME.</b> That claim was in
    the TB header, in the encoder, and in two WORKLOG lines,
    and it was wrong in all four.
    ...
    <b>It was the INSTRUMENT that was wrong</b> - the pattern
    this whole block exists to demonstrate - and it is why
    the act was written with <b>TWO decoders from the wire
    rules</b> rather than one decoder and one encoder that
    agree by construction. A pair that agrees by construction
    agrees even when both are wrong.
  end note
end group

== the decoder's actual job: compare with the last CHANGE ==

DEC -> TICK : read the counter
TICK -> DEC : now
DEC -> DEC : level == dmem[4] (the level at the last change)?
note over DEC
  <b>A BI-PHASE RECEIVER DOES NOT SYNCHRONISE ON A LEVEL.\b>
  ...
  It TIMESTAMPS the level and compares it with the level recorded at the last
  CHANGE, and it is that comparison which RECOVERS THE CLOCK. There is no other
  clock in the system: the one the receiver runs on is the one it takes from the
  data.
  ...
  <b>THE BOUNCE IS FORCED BY THE ISA.</b> The store-forward a transition detector
  wants, X = ram[X], cannot be written: <b>OP_LDS (0xB) is a <= ram[x] with NO X
  destination</b>, and the encoding that looks like it, LDM X,addr with arg[7]=1, is
  a <b>DIRECT</b> load whose address field is the <b>top nibble only</b> and cannot
  even name byte 8. So the old level lives in a memory byte -
  ...
  <b>and that byte belongs to this decoder and to NOTHING ELSE.</b> A scratch shared
  between a routine and its caller is how the DS18B20 act lost a byte counter to the
  delay routine.
end note
DEC -> DEC : no change: keep timestamping
DEC -> DEC : changed: dmem[8] += 1 half-interval

note over DEC
  <b>WHY THE BIT IS DECIDED ONCE PER BIT AND NOT ONCE PER
  TRANSITION.</b>
  ...
  A bit is TWO half-intervals, so counting transitions and calling each one a bit
  gives two bits where there is one. And the distinction that matters - <b>a data
  transition INSIDE a bit</b> - is only visible if the decoder is counting
  half-intervals rather than edges, because an edge is an edge wherever it falls.
  ...
  dmem[8] counts half-intervals and dmem[12,13] holds the bit under construction.
end note

== the enabled-flag trap, which is the sixth of its kind ==

note over ENC, DEC
  <b>PINOE = 0x00 RELEASES EVERY PAD, AND IN PARTICULAR THE INPUT.</b>
  ...
  A pad the firmware DRIVES reads back its own register through PIN, not the wire.
  <b>Claiming the input pad means the firmware never sees the stream at all.</b>
  ...
  The first version of this init CLAIMED the input - writing BMC_IN to PINOE, on the
  reasoning that "the input is the device's, so claim it" - and the symptom was that
  <b>NO TRANSITION WAS EVER DETECTED</b>, which reads as a decoder that does not work
  rather than as an enable written backwards.
  ...
  <b>It is the SIXTH time in this block that a level and an enable have been
  confused,</b> and the only reason it was found at all is that the testbench's
  check NAMES THE FLAG and the flag was 0xFF.
end note

== the one input the act must be able to FAIL on ==

note over ENC, DEC
  <b>A LINE HELD CONSTANT IS THE INPUT THAT MATTERS MOST.</b>
  ...
  <b>EVERY</b> bit of a well-formed frame carries a transition in its middle,
  because "bi-phase" means the level changes WITHIN the interval. So a line held
  CONSTANT - which is what a disconnected, shorted or dead sensor looks like on the
  wire - has <b>no transitions at all and carries no clock</b>, and a receiver that
  locked on to it has locked on to nothing.
  ...
  THAT is the stream the last check presents, and the firmware's answer to it must be
  a <b>DECLARED FAILURE (dmem[3] = 0xFF) rather than a byte it invented.</b>
  ...
  <b>A DECODER THAT CANNOT FAIL IS NOT A DECODER.</b>
end note

== the design's timing constants, and what is NOT measured ==

note over ENC, DEC
  |= quantity |= value | source | measured? |=
  | half-interval | <b>2 us</b> = 120 clocks | the encoder's counted delay | NO - act is RED |
  | bit period | <b>4 us</b> | two half-intervals | NO |
  | the counter's unit | <b>1 us</b> = 60 clocks | port 0x4, I2CTICK | n/a - it is the SoC's |
  | a 1's extra transition, FM0 | at the <b>START</b> of the interval | the wire rules | NO |
  | a 1's transitions, FM1 | <b>none</b> inside the bit | the wire rules | NO |
  | a 0's transition | at the <b>END</b>, both encodings | the wire rules | NO |
  ...
  <b>NOTHING IN THIS TABLE HAS BEEN MEASURED,</b> and the figure says so in its own
  title. Every other timing figure in this set carries measured numbers from a
  passing act; this one carries the DESIGN's targets, which is what the seven RED
  checks are asserting against.
  ...
  <b>What is NOT modelled, and is stated rather than buried:</b> one inter-frame gap,
  and <b>no noise, no jitter and no line-length limit</b>. Those are the things a real
  receiver's worst case lives in, and pretending otherwise would be claiming a
  robustness this act does not test.
end note

@enduml
